      "startLine": 1,
      "code": "package net.terminalvalue.crosscut.retry;\n\nimport java.util.Random;\n\n/**\n * Full jitter: a retry waits a time drawn uniformly from nothing up to the exponential backoff's delay, so that the\n * clients one failure released together do not come back together (Brooker, \"Exponential Backoff And Jitter\",\n * 2015; Resilience4j's {@code IntervalFunction.ofExponentialRandomBackoff}). The draw is seeded by the request and\n * the attempt, so that a test, or a replay of a request log, sees the waits the service saw.\n */\npublic final class Jitter {\n\n    private Jitter() {}\n\n    /**\n     * The wait before the given attempt of a request, in milliseconds: at least zero and less than the ceiling, the\n     * base doubled {@code attempt} times and stopping at the cap. The doubling is a shift, and it stops one short of\n     * the base's sign bit, the base's leading zeros less one, so that the ceiling stays positive however many\n     * attempts there were; the cap is reached long before that for any policy in use.\n     */\n    public static int fullJitterMillis(int baseMillis, int capMillis, int attempt, long requestId) {\n        if (baseMillis <= 0 || capMillis < baseMillis || attempt < 0) {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"the base must be positive, the cap at least the base, the attempt not negative\");\n        }\n        int shift = Math.min(attempt, Integer.numberOfLeadingZeros(baseMillis) - 1);\n        int ceiling = Math.min(capMillis, baseMillis << shift);\n        return new Random(requestId * 31 + attempt).nextInt(ceiling);\n    }\n}\n"
    },
